XVI. Numantia’s Constitution

I would give it seven laws.

1. Relation before coincidence.
A visual resemblance isn’t enough.

2. Name the invariant.
If you can’t say what survived, you haven’t finished mining.

3. State the listening lens.
All rhyme occurs relative to some representation or property.

4. Prefer generative patterns.
The strongest pattern predicts or produces unseen instances.

5. Test against null worlds.
Ask how frequently the apparent rhyme would occur accidentally.

6. Broken rhyme is information.
Exceptions are not garbage; they may reveal another layer.

7. Translate structure, not surface.
The highest-value patterns survive a substrate change.

That keeps the entire civilization epistemically sane.

